   "t": 1791256869.838774,
   "kind": "assistant_text",
   "text": "## Summary\n\nI built the trading specification using a greedy search process under the content-move budget:\n\n1. **Declared stopping rules** upfront: stop if best-so-far Sharpe > 1.2, stop if \u22652 failed moves, or stop if the last gain is \u22640.02.\n2. **`init`**: anchored on feature **29 (long)** \u2192 in-sample Sharpe 0.3093.\n3. **`extend_best`**: added feature **20 (long)** \u2192 Sharpe improved to 0.4077.\n4. **`extend_best`**: added feature **28 (long)** \u2192 Sharpe jumped to **0.6425**.\n\nThat exhausted my 3-move budget (init + 2 extends), so I stopped there with the final specification:\n\n**Spec: equal-weight long [Feature 29, Feature 20, Feature 28]**\n**In-sample Sharpe (net of costs): 0.6425**\n\n**Submitted prediction:** out-of-sample Sharpe mean = **0.38**, sd = **0.30**\n\nRationale for the discount: each step searched across ~76\u201380 candidate features, which is a fairly wide search even with just 3 moves \u2014 meaning meaningful in-sample selection bias is likely baked into the 0.64 figure. I discounted roughly 40% from the in-sample estimate to account for this overfitting risk, while keeping a wide standard deviation (0.30) to reflect genuine uncertainty about how much of the in-sample edge is real signal versus noise from the feature search."
